A||||15||zeusrekning||Dad wants to know the biggest v6 he can put in my fiero||||1||
Z||000000||zeusrekning||12-22-2000||12:31 PM||||He wants to know what is the biggest v6 that will bolt to the fiero transaxle. I told him I thought the 3.8. He wants an engine that is carberated and that you can get alot of high perf. parts for.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||12-22-2000||12:46 PM||||The biggest is a 4.3L using a V-8 install kit. If going carb'd I go with a 3.8L for easy access to parts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-22-2000||01:11 PM||||Zeus,<P>The 4.3L is a 350 with two cylinders hacked off.  This is great for performance builds because, pistons, con rods, bearings, rocker arms.... etc. are readily available.  I am not sure if GM ever produced these carbed though.  <P>Of course the 3.8 can be impressive but you would have to go waaaaay back to find a carbed one.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000003||lou_dias||12-22-2000||02:44 PM||||Actually, it's the 4.5... a bored out 4.3...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tryxalon||12-22-2000||03:43 PM||||I didn't think the 3.8 or 4.3 would bolt up to the Fiero Transaxle without adapters? <P>Wouldn't the largest then be the 3.4 OHV 60 degree v-6?  And, wouldn't that be large enough?? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||RiceCooker||12-22-2000||03:59 PM||||If you go for the 3.8 than all you need is one high performance part - the supercharger!<BR>I cannot speak from experience but i'm sure that some of the forum members could convince you and your pops to go for the s/c instead of carburating.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tom88gt||12-22-2000||04:01 PM||||Any engine will bolt to any trans.  Just not directly.  A company called KEP makes countless adapter plates for any engine imaginable to VW and Porsche transaxles.  They probably make just about all the other cars too.  Like all the fiero kits.  They just make the adapters and flywheels.  Engine swappers use KEP adapters in their kits and resell them to you.  Plus they add all the wiring, motor mounts, and other custom items.<P>That said, 4.3 Chevy is the biggest V-6 I know of.  They are not carbed but there are carb intakes abd such available from Summit, PAW and such.<P>The Buick 3.8 was also offered as a 4.1 and numerous other sizes.  The 3800 is a newer generation of the 3.8 series.  The 3.8/4.1 are available carbed.  Also has the most performance goodies available (due to very long production history).  Turbos available in junkyard for cheap.<P>Any engine can be made bigger by boring and stroking (pistons are cheap, stroker cranks are not).||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||12-23-2000||12:18 AM||||At .060 over a 4.3 is  a 4.43L and the one we had still ran like crap. If you get one make sure it's a vortec. Otherwise, just get a 3800.||||reg||1
